1
00:00:06,670 --> 00:00:10,170
Greetings and welcome back to the third week

2
00:00:10,170 --> 00:00:14,490
of the MOC introduction to Biarisk Management.

3
00:00:14,490 --> 00:00:15,970
In this lecture module,

4
00:00:15,970 --> 00:00:20,070
I will introduce you to the concept of the controls,

5
00:00:20,070 --> 00:00:22,530
as well as the hierarchy of controls.

6
00:00:22,530 --> 00:00:24,730
And I will describe the manner

7
00:00:24,730 --> 00:00:28,010
in which the controls can be applied concurrently

8
00:00:28,010 --> 00:00:30,690
to mitigate or reduce the risk of exposure

9
00:00:30,690 --> 00:00:36,450
to the biological agent during laboratory operations.

10
00:00:36,470 --> 00:00:41,430
The extensive description of each of these controls

11
00:00:41,430 --> 00:00:44,270
will be discussed in the lectures which follow

12
00:00:44,270 --> 00:00:49,350
during this current week.

13
00:00:49,350 --> 00:00:51,390
These are your learning objectives.

14
00:00:51,390 --> 00:00:54,190
The first learning objective is to introduce you

15
00:00:54,190 --> 00:00:58,310
to the concept of the control or the biarisk control.

16
00:00:59,390 --> 00:01:02,650
The second objective is the hierarchy of the controls.

17
00:01:02,650 --> 00:01:05,470
And the third is to give you a brief overview

18
00:01:05,470 --> 00:01:09,740
of each of the controls.

19
00:01:09,740 --> 00:01:12,360
What you should be able to do upon completion

20
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:16,960
of this module, describe the concept of the control,

21
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:20,120
describe the five controls and the hierarchy,

22
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:23,040
and identify situations in which controls

23
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:29,170
can be applied concurrently.

24
00:01:29,170 --> 00:01:32,650
This is the specific definition for biarisk controls,

25
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:37,530
which has been adapted from ISO IEC guide.

26
00:01:38,530 --> 00:01:43,130
Actions implementing biarisk management decisions

27
00:01:43,130 --> 00:01:49,600
are termed as biarisk controls.

28
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:54,600
Let us look back briefly on this description of the flow.

29
00:01:56,360 --> 00:01:59,120
So we commence with hazard identification.

30
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:01,360
We identify the hazards.

31
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:04,320
We then conduct a risk assessment

32
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:07,420
and categorize our biological agent

33
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:09,560
into the respective risk group.

34
00:02:09,580 --> 00:02:13,340
We also conduct a risk assessment using a risk matrix

35
00:02:13,340 --> 00:02:16,020
as was discussed earlier.

36
00:02:16,020 --> 00:02:17,900
We then move on to risk mitigation

37
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:21,380
and this is where the controls come into the picture.

38
00:02:23,140 --> 00:02:26,060
Finally, we have what is known as residual risk.

39
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:29,220
And it is at this point that you make this decision,

40
00:02:29,220 --> 00:02:33,860
should I proceed with the laboratory procedure

41
00:02:33,860 --> 00:02:35,260
or should I not proceed?

42
00:02:35,260 --> 00:02:38,060
So if the residual risk is too high

43
00:02:38,160 --> 00:02:39,720
or not acceptable,

44
00:02:39,720 --> 00:02:43,980
you do not continue with this particular procedure.

45
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:53,560
If it's acceptable, you may continue with the procedure.

46
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:56,600
We will look at the five controls and the hierarchy

47
00:02:56,600 --> 00:03:01,140
and I will describe these controls in detail.

48
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:03,440
The five controls are elimination,

49
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:11,460
substitution, engineering controls,

50
00:03:11,460 --> 00:03:14,140
administrative controls,

51
00:03:14,140 --> 00:03:18,580
and personal protective equipment.

52
00:03:18,580 --> 00:03:21,100
Personal protective equipment

53
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:24,060
provide the last line of defense

54
00:03:24,060 --> 00:03:26,780
against the biological agent.

55
00:03:26,780 --> 00:03:28,700
This is why they are placed right

56
00:03:28,700 --> 00:03:34,920
at the bottom of this hierarchy.

57
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:40,710
I will describe each of these controls in detail.

58
00:03:40,710 --> 00:03:44,570
The first one is elimination.

59
00:03:44,570 --> 00:03:46,330
Let us look at this situation.

60
00:03:47,890 --> 00:03:51,010
Your laboratory has been ordered to test samples

61
00:03:51,010 --> 00:03:53,590
which are likely to belong to risk group four.

62
00:03:53,590 --> 00:03:56,870
This may happen in a situation such as a pandemic

63
00:03:56,870 --> 00:04:00,610
in which you are ordered to test pathogens

64
00:04:00,610 --> 00:04:04,090
or biological agents which belong to a high risk group,

65
00:04:04,090 --> 00:04:05,450
which is risk group four.

66
00:04:05,450 --> 00:04:07,690
However, your laboratory is not equipped

67
00:04:07,690 --> 00:04:09,130
with directional airflow.

68
00:04:09,130 --> 00:04:11,370
You do not have a containment facility.

69
00:04:11,370 --> 00:04:13,810
The staff do not have adequate training

70
00:04:13,810 --> 00:04:16,430
and SOPs for waste decontamination

71
00:04:16,430 --> 00:04:18,350
and disposal do not exist.

72
00:04:18,350 --> 00:04:20,510
So you have multiple limitations.

73
00:04:20,510 --> 00:04:23,570
In this case, it is highly likely

74
00:04:23,570 --> 00:04:27,110
that there will be a breach of containment.

75
00:04:27,110 --> 00:04:30,750
And this is when we resort to elimination.

76
00:04:30,750 --> 00:04:33,910
We make a collective decision not to proceed

77
00:04:33,910 --> 00:04:40,920
with the laboratory procedure.

78
00:04:40,920 --> 00:04:47,040
The next control is substitution.

79
00:04:47,040 --> 00:04:49,080
Let us look at another situation.

80
00:04:49,080 --> 00:04:51,360
Your laboratory has been ordered to test samples

81
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:53,920
which are likely to belong to risk group four.

82
00:04:53,920 --> 00:04:57,020
This is the same situation as the first one.

83
00:04:57,020 --> 00:05:00,420
However, the samples will be inactivated

84
00:05:00,420 --> 00:05:02,580
prior to arrival in your laboratory,

85
00:05:02,580 --> 00:05:05,940
which means that they will be no longer viable

86
00:05:05,940 --> 00:05:09,900
and incapable of infecting laboratory workers.

87
00:05:09,900 --> 00:05:13,460
Your laboratory is equipped with the essential equipment,

88
00:05:13,460 --> 00:05:16,380
which is a biological safety cabinet.

89
00:05:16,380 --> 00:05:19,620
And the staff are competent and have conducted work

90
00:05:19,620 --> 00:05:23,300
with biological agents categorized

91
00:05:23,300 --> 00:05:26,220
as risk group four previously.

92
00:05:26,220 --> 00:05:31,060
Now, all of these factors add to the adequacy

93
00:05:31,060 --> 00:05:32,660
of your controls.

94
00:05:32,660 --> 00:05:35,220
And in this case, you may proceed

95
00:05:35,220 --> 00:05:40,930
with the laboratory procedure with caution.

96
00:05:40,930 --> 00:05:42,970
This is a case of substitution.

97
00:05:42,970 --> 00:05:45,890
So the very fact that the sample was inactivated

98
00:05:45,890 --> 00:05:48,730
reduced the likelihood of exposure

99
00:05:48,730 --> 00:05:50,730
to the biological agent

100
00:05:50,730 --> 00:05:56,310
and the subsequent consequences as well.

101
00:05:56,310 --> 00:05:58,190
The third control, which we will look at

102
00:05:58,190 --> 00:06:00,030
is the engineering control.

103
00:06:00,070 --> 00:06:02,470
And in order to understand engineering controls,

104
00:06:02,470 --> 00:06:04,790
you need to have a basic understanding

105
00:06:04,790 --> 00:06:11,100
of the layout of your respective laboratory.

106
00:06:11,100 --> 00:06:13,500
This is a conceptual laboratory,

107
00:06:13,500 --> 00:06:15,340
which is an enclosed space.

108
00:06:15,340 --> 00:06:17,780
It's just a physical enclosure.

109
00:06:17,780 --> 00:06:20,460
Now, let us say that your management decides

110
00:06:20,460 --> 00:06:25,460
to upgrade this to a BSL-2 or a BSL-3 laboratory.

111
00:06:25,460 --> 00:06:27,180
The first thing that you need to look at

112
00:06:27,180 --> 00:06:29,380
is the effluent disposal system,

113
00:06:29,380 --> 00:06:32,060
which is responsible for the disposal

114
00:06:32,060 --> 00:06:33,860
of all the wastes and liquids

115
00:06:33,860 --> 00:06:36,100
coming out from your facility.

116
00:06:36,100 --> 00:06:38,860
Then you look at the HEPA filtration system.

117
00:06:38,860 --> 00:06:41,340
Now, this also adds to your facility

118
00:06:41,340 --> 00:06:43,700
as you will have to develop

119
00:06:43,700 --> 00:06:46,820
or design two additional floors,

120
00:06:46,820 --> 00:06:51,220
which is to house the HEPA air filtration system

121
00:06:51,220 --> 00:06:53,620
and your effluent disposal system.

122
00:06:53,620 --> 00:06:56,180
So a lab basically gets converted

123
00:06:56,180 --> 00:06:58,020
into a three-story facility.

124
00:06:59,620 --> 00:07:01,740
Then you have your biological safety cabinet,

125
00:07:01,740 --> 00:07:03,900
which is an essential equipment.

126
00:07:03,900 --> 00:07:06,380
This biological agent must be hard ducted

127
00:07:06,380 --> 00:07:08,580
into the HEPA filtration system

128
00:07:08,580 --> 00:07:12,620
and the exhaust released into the environment

129
00:07:12,620 --> 00:07:14,060
after filtration.

130
00:07:15,860 --> 00:07:18,260
You can now bring in your personnel

131
00:07:18,260 --> 00:07:20,980
and your animals in this facility,

132
00:07:20,980 --> 00:07:25,030
your experimental animals,

133
00:07:25,030 --> 00:07:28,230
and your directional airflow is set into place

134
00:07:28,230 --> 00:07:33,240
as well as the effluent disposal system.

135
00:07:33,240 --> 00:07:34,760
So you have directional airflow,

136
00:07:34,920 --> 00:07:37,360
air which enters the facility is filtered

137
00:07:37,360 --> 00:07:40,360
and the pressure within this facility is negative

138
00:07:40,360 --> 00:07:43,640
with reference to the external environment.

139
00:07:43,640 --> 00:07:46,920
This ensures that there is always a flow of air

140
00:07:46,920 --> 00:07:49,120
into the facility and all the air

141
00:07:49,120 --> 00:07:52,600
which exits the facility is basically filtered.

142
00:07:53,920 --> 00:07:56,960
This represents a containment zone,

143
00:07:56,960 --> 00:07:59,800
which offers secondary containment.

144
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,600
However, your personnel must also ensure

145
00:08:03,600 --> 00:08:06,520
that they don't proper protective equipment

146
00:08:06,520 --> 00:08:08,360
or personal protective equipment

147
00:08:08,360 --> 00:08:11,080
in order to work in this facility.

148
00:08:11,080 --> 00:08:14,920
So this is a brief description of the engineering control.

149
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:18,040
I will discuss the engineering control in detail

150
00:08:18,040 --> 00:08:24,140
in a subsequent lecture.

151
00:08:24,140 --> 00:08:26,420
The engineering design is basically governed

152
00:08:26,420 --> 00:08:28,100
by the facility design itself.

153
00:08:28,100 --> 00:08:30,540
This is where engineers come in.

154
00:08:30,540 --> 00:08:33,420
Then you have your directional airflow,

155
00:08:33,420 --> 00:08:36,020
your filtration systems,

156
00:08:36,020 --> 00:08:38,940
your heating ventilation and air conditioning system,

157
00:08:38,940 --> 00:08:41,700
the effluent disposal system,

158
00:08:41,700 --> 00:08:43,660
the plumbing systems,

159
00:08:43,660 --> 00:08:45,420
the intelligent controls,

160
00:08:45,420 --> 00:08:48,900
which may involve computers and other devices

161
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:52,660
which link your laboratory to the network

162
00:08:52,660 --> 00:08:55,260
and which you can monitor and control

163
00:08:55,260 --> 00:08:57,980
from a remote hosting station,

164
00:08:57,980 --> 00:09:00,180
as well as the security systems

165
00:09:00,180 --> 00:09:03,380
which are responsible for the management

166
00:09:03,380 --> 00:09:08,710
of biosecurity at your respective facility.

167
00:09:08,710 --> 00:09:11,870
We now move on to administrative controls.

168
00:09:11,870 --> 00:09:13,430
So the administrative controls

169
00:09:13,430 --> 00:09:15,950
basically comprise the policies

170
00:09:15,950 --> 00:09:22,600
as well as the laboratory virus management system.

171
00:09:22,600 --> 00:09:24,640
We have our policies,

172
00:09:25,800 --> 00:09:27,520
roles and responsibilities

173
00:09:27,520 --> 00:09:31,160
as defined in the laboratory virus management manual.

174
00:09:31,160 --> 00:09:32,840
The standard operating procedures,

175
00:09:32,840 --> 00:09:34,360
these are very important

176
00:09:34,360 --> 00:09:36,800
and we will be discussing these in detail

177
00:09:36,800 --> 00:09:39,040
in a separate lecture.

178
00:09:39,040 --> 00:09:42,520
Contingency plans which address emergencies,

179
00:09:42,520 --> 00:09:44,560
vaccination of personnel,

180
00:09:45,600 --> 00:09:47,240
training of personnel

181
00:09:47,240 --> 00:09:55,810
and audits which form part of your performance assessment.

182
00:09:55,810 --> 00:09:57,610
We now move on to the fifth control

183
00:09:57,610 --> 00:09:59,170
which is the last line of defense

184
00:09:59,170 --> 00:10:01,090
against the biological agent

185
00:10:01,090 --> 00:10:06,720
and this is personal protective equipment.

186
00:10:06,720 --> 00:10:08,800
These include gloves,

187
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:10,440
different kinds of gloves

188
00:10:10,440 --> 00:10:13,800
based on the type of the laboratory procedure.

189
00:10:13,800 --> 00:10:15,680
For example, when working with animals,

190
00:10:15,680 --> 00:10:18,200
you may have to wear special gloves

191
00:10:18,200 --> 00:10:22,850
which prevent bites and scratches.

192
00:10:22,850 --> 00:10:25,010
Masks, respirators.

193
00:10:25,010 --> 00:10:26,370
Respirators are masks

194
00:10:26,370 --> 00:10:29,450
which are fitted with motorized HEPA filters.

195
00:10:29,450 --> 00:10:30,970
I will discuss this in detail

196
00:10:30,970 --> 00:10:33,930
and provide you with a demonstration of a respirator.

197
00:10:35,610 --> 00:10:39,330
Protective gowns which protect your skin

198
00:10:39,330 --> 00:10:44,170
from cutaneous infection via the skin

199
00:10:44,170 --> 00:10:46,450
which may be in the form of spills.

200
00:10:46,450 --> 00:10:49,410
Protective glasses which protect your eyes

201
00:10:49,410 --> 00:10:52,530
from viruses which may enter through the ocular route.

202
00:10:53,570 --> 00:10:56,330
So these are some of the personal protective equipment

203
00:10:56,330 --> 00:11:02,740
which I will discuss in a subsequent lecture.

204
00:11:02,740 --> 00:11:05,860
Finally, we move on to the concurrent usage of controls.

205
00:11:07,100 --> 00:11:09,660
So controls are used concurrently.

206
00:11:09,660 --> 00:11:11,940
For instance, your personal protective equipment

207
00:11:11,940 --> 00:11:13,420
must be used concurrently

208
00:11:13,420 --> 00:11:15,940
with the standard operating procedure

209
00:11:15,940 --> 00:11:19,420
for donning and doffing of the PPE.

210
00:11:19,420 --> 00:11:23,140
So the SOP is essentially an administrative control.

211
00:11:23,140 --> 00:11:28,140
Entry and exit into the laboratory is governed by an SOP.

212
00:11:28,140 --> 00:11:31,020
The laboratory in itself is an engineering control.

213
00:11:31,020 --> 00:11:33,620
However, the entry and exit is governed

214
00:11:33,620 --> 00:11:35,940
by an administrative control.

215
00:11:35,940 --> 00:11:39,780
The SOP for the operation of the entire facility itself

216
00:11:39,780 --> 00:11:42,700
is governed by more than one control.

217
00:11:42,700 --> 00:11:45,460
This is how the controls are used concurrently.

218
00:11:45,460 --> 00:11:47,700
So you may have administrative controls,

219
00:11:47,700 --> 00:11:49,820
engineering controls and the PPEs

220
00:11:49,820 --> 00:11:52,380
all overlapping with each other.

221
00:11:52,380 --> 00:11:54,740
And this is the way in which we manage

222
00:11:54,740 --> 00:11:57,820
the bi-risk management system

223
00:11:57,820 --> 00:12:02,220
in a specific containment facility.

224
00:12:02,220 --> 00:12:05,460
This brings us to the end of this introductory module

225
00:12:05,460 --> 00:12:07,020
on risk mitigation.

226
00:12:07,020 --> 00:12:08,980
We have learned about the five controls.

227
00:12:08,980 --> 00:12:11,420
We have learned about the hierarchy of controls,

228
00:12:11,420 --> 00:12:13,660
the concept of residual risk,

229
00:12:13,660 --> 00:12:17,100
as well as the concurrent usage of controls.

230
00:12:17,100 --> 00:12:19,660
I will delve into each of these controls

231
00:12:19,660 --> 00:12:24,380
in a series of lectures during this current week.

232
00:12:24,380 --> 00:12:27,020
Thank you very much for your participation

233
00:12:27,020 --> 00:12:28,860
in this lecture module.

234
00:12:28,860 --> 00:12:33,870
And I wish you a pleasant learning experience.

235
00:12:33,870 --> 00:12:35,790
Thank you and stay bio safe.
